title: Job 42: The Latter End of Job
description: The Job class, part 5 of 5: Job repents in dust and ashes, the LORD's wrath against Eliphaz and his two friends, Job prays for them, the captivity of Job turned, and his latter end blessed more than his beginning, from the Job 42 studies.
output: lessons/job/job-42-the-latter-end.html

# The Job class, part 5 of 5. Slide text is the author's own sentences from the Job 42 studies
# (studies/data/books/Job.json: 17061, 17062, 17138), with "Doing the will of God from the heart" (10526) and
# "Retribution theology is man's, not God's" (16853). Verses typed in the studies are shown from the KJV.
# Fixed: "gained the the understanding" -> "the understanding", "Jobs delight" -> "Job's delight" (17061);
# "Where we there when He placed the stars" -> "Were we there" (10526);
# Left as written, to ask: "like Lot's children, male or female, we are made joint heirs" (17138) may mean Job's children.

=== Introduction
--- Job 42 [title]
# image: lessons/job/job-42-the-latter-end.jpg (shadow-play title art, to be added)
overline: The Job class · Part 5 of 5
heading: Job 42: The Latter End of Job
sub: Our later end will always be greater than our former, because we are all heirs in Jesus.
refs: Job 42:5-6; Job 42:10; Job 42:12
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

--- Where we are
heading: Where we are: Part 5, Job 42
tile: 1–2 | The trial begins
tile: 3–31 | Job and his three friends
tile: 32–37 | Elihu speaks
tile: 38–41 | Out of the whirlwind
tile: 42 | The latter end of Job
p: Job isn't restored by his own wisdom, his works, by proving his own righteousness before God. When the Lord offered Job to gird up his loins and confront Him, Job could only cover his mouth and sit in silence. So, it is God who restores Job.

=== Now mine eye seeth thee
# from 10526
--- For who are we?
kicker: Job 42
lead: For who are we? Were we there when He placed the stars in the heavens and made the leviathan? When the lightning was formed and the thunder of His wrath was made?
p: God, appearing in a whirlwind to Job asks similar questions of righteous Job. He asks these things of the created who is questioning his Creator.
p: And Job, having been subject to this glory of God and His Majesty, replies;
read: app:doctrine-family~10526 | Doing the will of God from the heart

# from 17061
--- I abhor myself, and repent in dust and ashes
kicker: Job 42
scripture: Job 42:1-6 | mark: therefore have I uttered that I understood not; but now mine eye seeth thee; repent in dust and ashes
p: Job, having seen the glory of God, His power and might, he says he has uttered what he understood not. In ignorance, he demanded of God the "why" to his predicament, and now sees the Who behind all things. He has gained understanding and repented towards the truth of the power of God. Left humbled in the dust and ash of his mourning.
take: This settled, Job has gained the understanding that God does what He does, at a higher purpose and longer view than His creation can even comprehend.

=== My servant Job shall pray for you
# from 17061, 16853
--- Ye have not spoken of me the thing that is right
kicker: Job 42
scripture: Job 42:7-9 | mark: My wrath is kindled against thee, and against thy two friends; ye have not spoken of me the thing that is right, as my servant Job hath; my servant Job shall pray for you
panel: Eliphaz | the Temanite
panel: Bildad | the Shuhite
panel: Zophar | the Naamathite
p: The Lord then turns to Eliphaz and the two friends, saying they kindled His wrath, having spoken falsely about Him. Things that aren't right. Their prosperity gospel theology and their standing in judgment as if they were God Himself against Job was wrong.

# from 16853
--- Human nature, but not God's
kicker: Job 42
p: The friends of Job, (and later, the young accuser Elihu son of Barachel the Buzite), are given opportunity to make multiple philosophical and theological points regarding the nature of God, His justice and their natural suspicion of those who suffer as being sinners, and worse, that those that suffer worse than they do must be greater sinners than they are.
p: This line of thought is just as common today as it was then, and is human nature. But not God's. And while there is general wisdom to be found in their arguments, the application they choose to make is wrong.
read: app:doctrine-trust~16853 | Retribution theology is man's, not God's

# from 17061
--- Job the mediator
kicker: Job 42
p: And He tells them to make sacrifices and offer burnt offerings, and Job's prayers for forgiveness for them He will accept. This act of humiliation, seeking forgiveness through the man they had slandered, is done according to God's word, and the Lord accepted Job.
callout: This, in a way is symbolic of Christ as the mediator between God and man, as Job is the mediator here for his friends.

# from 17061
--- When he prayed for his friends
kicker: Job 42
big: Job 42:10 | mark: the LORD turned the captivity of Job, when he prayed for his friends
p: And when Job prayed for his friends, the Lord turned the captivity of Job, the allowances of the devil to control his fate, and restored Job twice as much as he had.
p: In all these things Job never spoke improperly, but in ignorance demanded of God. But it was Job's forgiveness of others who had wronged him that led God to restore him, and bless him with more than he had.
take: Job reached peace with God just by his understanding and faith. But it was forgiveness of others that restored him. This is such an important point to remember.

=== The latter end of Job
# from 17061
--- The latter end more than his beginning
kicker: Job 42
scripture: Job 42:11-12 | mark: comforted him over all the evil; So the LORD blessed the latter end of Job more than his beginning
p: Job first receives the comforting and support, from family and friends, that he needed. And their financial support to him that was without, allowed Job to restore his wealth. The love and respect Job had shown others paid off, as they loved on him with emotional support and alms. Gold and riches he used to make his flocks great again.

# from 17061
--- Inheritance among their brethren
kicker: Job 42
scripture: Job 42:13-15 | mark: Jemima; Kezia; Kerenhappuch; their father gave them inheritance among their brethren
p: And God blessed him with 10 children, seven sons and 3 daughters. And Job's delight in his daughters, naming them for peace, and fragrant spice and beauty, led him to make them co-heirs as their brothers.

# from 17062
--- Old and full of days
kicker: Job 42
scripture: Job 42:16-17 | mark: even four generations; So Job died, being old and full of days
p: The Lord gave Job a long life, to see four generations come from these children. And so Job died, being old and full of days. Quite a ways away from his lamenting his own birth ([[Job 3:3]]).

=== Our latter end
# from 17062
--- To better know God
p: Our struggles and temptations may never be as horrific and total as Job's test. We may seek to know why from the Lord. We may be tempted to blame Him, to curse God and die, as Job's wife said. Others may use the occasion to imply we deserve it.
lead: But we should see the opportunity to better know God. To grow our faith, and our understanding of Him. To know that here, or in the resurrection, we shall overcome.

# from 17062
--- Greater than our former
p: The Lord will deliver us, He has turned the captivity, the bondage of sin we are all under, to liberty in Christ. We have the Comforter sent by Him. Our later end will always be greater than our former, because we are all heirs in Jesus.
big: Matthew 6:33 | mark: seek ye first the kingdom of God, and his righteousness
center: Amen.

# from 17138
--- His (and our) Redeemer
p: Like Job, we are born of woman and blessed by God to an abundant life, no matter our station, having life. When temptation comes, and the law enters, and sin slays us, the Accuser, Satan, does his worst to us (tempered by God's mercy).
p: We have trials, and tribulations. But, entering into grace by faith, we have the hope and promise of God in Christ that our end will be better than our beginning. That at the adoption, when we put on immortality and shed corruption, when we are redeemed, our end will be glorious, as we will be like Him. And like Lot's children, male or female, we are made joint heirs with Christ.

=== What we have seen
--- What we have seen
point: Why, and Who | In ignorance, he demanded of God the "why" to his predicament, and now sees the Who behind all things.
point: The friends' error | Their prosperity gospel theology and their standing in judgment as if they were God Himself against Job was wrong.
point: The mediator | Symbolic of Christ as the mediator between God and man, as Job is the mediator here for his friends.
point: Forgiveness | Job reached peace with God just by his understanding and faith. But it was forgiveness of others that restored him.
point: The latter end | Our later end will always be greater than our former, because we are all heirs in Jesus.
